[vr overlay R8] The VR aim reticle: aim it where the camera is looking, and draw
it as its own bright mark instead of as a particle.

Reported in the headset on 1.0.11.10: "the VR crosshair does not show anywhere".
It was added every frame — proven from the headset itself, because the flat 2D
crosshair is suppressed only when dot_drawn is 1 and that one was missing too.
Four faults, and the simulator found the two that actually hid it:

  1. THE RAY WAS IN THE WRONG FRAME, which is what put the mark outside the
     view. `hand_ray_ang` carries the play-space body yaw; the RENDER adds the
     server's own re-orientation on top of it (view.c: viewangles[YAW] +
     server_yaw when yaw_relative), and every Quake II spawn sets
     delta_angles[YAW] to the spawn angle. On base1 the two differ by 135
     degrees. Measured with the draw instrumented: the reticle's world point
     came back 119 units along +x while the camera faced (-0.71, +0.71).
  2. IT WAS A PARTICLE, and a particle cannot be a reticle. Driven to
     `gl_partscale 200` the sprite covers a third of the eye image, so it was
     never depth-failing and never culled — but its peak contribution over the
     background is about a third of its own colour, and at eight times size it
     smears into a sliver rather than growing into a mark. TEXNUM_PARTICLE is a
     soft radially-faded blob meant to be one of hundreds in a spray. The
     reticle now carries its own world points in q2vr and the refresh draws them
     itself (GL_DrawVRReticle, tess.c): white texture, full alpha, depth test
     off — which is what every shipped VR crosshair does, because one hidden
     behind the player's own gun barrel is not doing its job.
  3. The call ran from CL_AddEntities, which V_RenderView reaches BEFORE view.c
     composes the VR head pose into cl.refdef.vieworg — so even a correctly
     aimed trace started at the pre-VR origin and was displaced by the whole
     room-scale offset. It moves to the end of the VR block in view.c.
  4. The standoff was max(1, range*0.004) — one unit below 250 units of range.
     It is now clamp(range*0.03, 6, 64); with the depth test off it is no longer
     what keeps the mark out of the wall, it is what keeps it at the right
     STEREO depth.

The mark is five points — a centre at a 0.92-degree angular size plus four
satellites at half that, 2.2 degrees out along the RAY's own right/up — so it
stays square to where the hand points and a busy wall texture cannot swallow it.

Verified on the Vision Pro simulator, in the eye image and not the UI texture
(the readback three rounds of reticle work were asserted against, where a world
particle can never appear): 3200 bright pixels in a compact 114x114 box centred
at (2399, 1351) of a 4800x2704 eye, peak luma +234 over the same frame with
`q2vrdot 0`. Suite case R8-5.

